+\title{Who Guards the Update? Transactional Publication Semantics for Continually Learning Embodied Agents}
 \author{Anonymous Author(s)}
 
 \begin{document}
 \maketitle
 
 \begin{abstract}
-An embodied agent can improve how it generates behavior while remaining unreliable at installing that behavior. A candidate controller may be useful in isolation yet acquire excessive actuator authority, inherit incompatible state, or overlap with commands from its predecessor. Action-level runtime assurance does not, by itself, specify this installation lifecycle. Our position is that, for embodied agents, adaptation should not imply deployment: proposed behavior should acquire authority over physical actuators through an explicit transactional installation boundary. We formulate requirements for that boundary, distinguish it from transactional physical execution, and use an incomplete runtime-programmable kernel as a case study. A retained synthetic verifier campaign supplies limited implementation evidence. Our main contribution is an evaluation agenda separating adaptation quality from update-system correctness. We report no continual-learning experiment; this position paper defines the systems problem and an experimental agenda for measuring it.
+An embodied agent can improve a controller while remaining unreliable at installing it. A candidate can pass isolated checks yet overlap an invocation of its predecessor, inherit incompatible state, or be activated against a stale version. Existing work already governs capability lifecycles and atomically hot-swaps robotic control laws. We therefore make a narrower claim: a runtime that exposes replaceable executable hooks should specify publication semantics at the invocation boundary. We describe one opt-in kernel TIMER slot with nonblocking, fail-closed replacement, explicit installation identity, restricted state reset, and resource-accounting updates committed with publication. The accompanying evaluation separates adaptation quality from installation correctness and compares three manager replacement protocols, with a separate snapshot-component ablation. We do not evaluate learning quality, downstream command fencing, hardware reset, or physical safety. Experiment results are pending validation.
 \end{abstract}
 
-\section{Adaptation should not imply deployment}
+\section{The update is part of the execution contract}
 \label{sec:position}
-Consider an agent that revises a motor reflex after a change in payload. The new controller passes its isolated tests. During replacement, however, a queued command from the old controller reaches the motor after the new version becomes active. Alternatively, the new code interprets its predecessor's integrator state differently. Either failure can occur even if the generated controller is useful and every command satisfies a local output limit. These are \emph{hypothetical installation failures}, not findings from a deployed learning robot. They expose a question distinct from whether the agent generated a better policy: \emph{how did that proposal become authoritative?}
+Consider an agent that revises a motor reflex after a payload change. The new controller passes isolated tests, but activation races an invocation of the old controller. A queued command from that invocation may reach the motor after the new controller becomes current. Alternatively, a stale recovery request may replace a later installation that happens to reuse the same program handle. These are installation failures, independent of whether the candidate is a better controller.
 
-Executable behavior is a concrete adaptation medium. Voyager grows a library of executable skills by refining candidate programs with environmental feedback in Minecraft \citep{wang2023voyager}. Physical control adds deadlines, restricted device access, and irreversible consequences to such a proposal loop. We focus on agents proposing bounded controller, reflex, or sensor-processing artifacts. We do not assume that foundation-model weights execute inside a small trusted runtime, or that verifying a program establishes the quality of the model that proposed it.
+Executable skills are one concrete adaptation medium: Voyager, for example, grows a library of programs using environmental feedback \citep{wang2023voyager}. Physical control adds restricted authority, deadlines, and effects that software rollback cannot undo. Our thesis is: \emph{adaptation should not imply deployment}. A candidate becomes authoritative only through a specified transition whose concurrency, identity, state, and accounting rules can be tested.
 
-Our position concerns the \textbf{proposal $\rightarrow$ authority transition}. Generating an update must not itself authorize installation, broaden device rights, or modify the mechanism that checks subsequent actions. The installation boundary should make those decisions explicit and observable. Its implementation could live in middleware, a runtime, or a kernel; the position does not require a new operating system.
+This thesis complements established mechanisms at adjacent layers. Qin et al. treat a new capability version as a governed candidate and define compatibility checks, sandbox and shadow evaluation, gated activation, monitoring, rollback, and audit \citep{qin2026governed}. LITHE loads a new C++ controller off the real-time path, then atomically swaps its function pointer at the end of one Spine cycle, where the single Spine loop supplies implicit quiescence; controller state is deliberately kept in a persistent shared-memory block \citep{lim2026lithe}. Dynamic software updating also supplies state-preserving program replacement, as in Kitsune \citep{hayden2014kitsune}. We focus beneath lifecycle policy and beside these update mechanisms: what should an opt-in kernel hook guarantee when concurrent invocation, resource ownership, and stale replacement requests are visible at that boundary?
+
+Action-level assurance remains separate. Shielding can constrain learner actions \citep{alshiekh2018shielding}, and Neural Simplex combines runtime assurance with online controller retraining \citep{phan2020neural}. An action monitor asks whether a command may execute in the current state. Publication asks which program installation is allowed to issue the next command. Either layer may reveal a failure the other cannot, so evaluations should report both rather than treating one as a proxy for the other.
+
+\section{A narrow transactional publication primitive}
+\label{sec:semantics}
+Our case study is an anonymous bare-metal Rust research kernel with verifier-accepted runtime programs, authenticated load policy, capability checks, quotas, generation-tagged handles, and immutable hook snapshots. We add one opt-in exclusive TIMER slot; ordinary hooks retain their existing behavior. The word \emph{transactional} refers only to publication of software authority at this slot. It does not imply atomic physical effects, persistent logging, or a general dynamic-update subsystem.
+
+An installation is identified by
+\begin{equation}
+ I=(h,e), \qquad e\in\{1,\ldots,2^{64}-1\},
+\end{equation}
+where $h$ is a program handle and $e$ is a monotonically increasing installation epoch. Replacement is a compare-and-swap-style operation on the expected $I$, not just $h$. It publishes a fresh epoch at every successful replacement, including rollback, so removing and later reinstalling the same handle does not make an old request current (the ABA case). Epoch exhaustion rejects the transition. Bootstrap is a separate operation.
+
+Invocation and replacement share one atomic state: open, active, or transitioning. An invocation atomically claims the open state; a transitioning state returns \textsc{TransitionBusy}, and an active state returns \textsc{ExecutionBusy}. Both failures increment explicit skipped-invocation counters. The invocation holds the immutable snapshot guard throughout execution and drops that guard before reopening the slot. A replacement tries to change open to transitioning once. If an invocation is active, it returns \textsc{Busy} immediately and leaves the slot unchanged; it never waits for quiescence.
+
+The only supported state policy is \textsc{Reset}, and only for candidates whose verifier-produced \texttt{referenced\_map\_handles} is empty. Map-bearing candidates return \textsc{PersistentStateUnsupported} before gate acquisition, and transfer is rejected explicitly. Thus ``reset'' means that no runtime map state is inherited; it says nothing about physical, device, or external state.
+
+Once gated and quiescent, replacement rechecks expected installation identity, ownership, authenticated load policy, verifier authority, conflicts, and quota. Fallible snapshot preparation precedes live mutation. A final emergency-stop check uses the kernel's existing monitor serialization; stop state or lock contention rejects without change. The commit publishes one old-to-new snapshot and fresh epoch together with a receipt and an allocation-free CPU-admission ledger delta of $-\mathrm{cost}(A)+\mathrm{cost}(B)$. Resident-memory charges for both programs persist. The previous program therefore remains available for rollback, which is the same conditional replacement in the reverse direction with a fresh epoch.
+
+The primitive deliberately stops at the executor boundary. Receipts and dispatch traces carry installation identity, enabling tests to correlate decisions with invocations. Extending that identity through downstream command queues and actuation audit records is future work. The present scope does not establish command freshness after dispatch, hardware-stop timing, physical safety, controller stability, or state migration.
+
+\section{Prototype and fault-injected publication experiment}
+\label{sec:evaluation}
+\subsection{Separate evaluation axes}
+The evaluation unit is a candidate within a versioned sequence. The task axis asks whether activated candidates improve an independently declared task metric. The installation axis asks whether the publication contract holds. These axes form four possible outcomes: improvement with or without correct installation, and regression with or without correct installation. A useful candidate cannot excuse an installation defect; correct publication cannot establish adaptation quality.
+
+We use hand-written, map-free bytecode candidates in hosted tests of the production kernel library. Fixtures explicitly enable unsigned development loading; the experiment does not test authentication. The embedded admission profile assigns a nominal 1\,kHz hook frequency and a 500\,ms/s aggregate CPU budget. Two unrelated syscall-hook programs consume budget without changing TIMER cardinality. These are admission-model quantities, not measured execution times.
+
+We compare three manager protocols on the same supported scenarios: \textbf{attach-first} (AF), \textbf{detach-first} (DF), and \textbf{transactional replacement} (TX). Each scenario fixes its starting installation, candidate bytecode, and fault location. Interrupting after the first publication leaves a two-step request incomplete; the one-publication TX request has already completed. Snapshot-allocation failure is injected immediately before candidate snapshot preparation, after predecessor removal for DF. Additional TX-only cases test an outstanding invocation, retry, and stale expected identity; they are excluded from the matched table.
+
+A separate \textbf{component ablation} publishes B through the existing epoch snapshot while retaining an A reader, then acquires a B reader before dropping A. It tests whether single-pointer publication alone prevents overlapping guards; it supplies neither manager accounting nor installation receipts. Host observations hold real production snapshot/invocation guards but do not execute privileged bytecode. We therefore report outstanding guards, not simultaneous motor commands. A QEMU probe separately exercises interpreter dispatch through A--B--A.
+
+Python analyzes Rust-emitted observations. Snapshot cardinality counts published program handles; accounting compares the observed ledger against independently calibrated fixture charges plus unrelated reservations. A failed request preserves its predecessor only if snapshot, installation identity, and ledger all remain unchanged. Table observations consist of actual intermediate snapshots and the final snapshot for each matched request; setup and restoration are excluded. These finite, deliberately adversarial schedules expose contract failures, not population failure rates.
 
 \begin{center}
-\fbox{\parbox{0.94\linewidth}{\textbf{Transactional update.} A software publication protocol in which a candidate is admitted, staged, atomically made authoritative, and retained or replaced according to explicit failure rules. This does \emph{not} imply transactional physical execution: motion that already occurred cannot be rolled back.}}
+\fbox{\parbox{0.92\linewidth}{\centering\textit{Experiment results pending validation.} No timing, count, rate, or comparative outcome is claimed in this draft.}}
 \end{center}
 
-The claim is testable: under matched candidate sequences and action assurance, installation controls should eliminate specified authority, version, state-transition, and resource failures. If an existing assurance stack already excludes these failures, or the controls add cost without improving the specified correctness measures, the additional boundary has not justified itself in that setting.
+The artifact retains the source revision and patch, commands, source hashes, raw JSONL, test logs, environment, derived CSV, and checksums. Additional tests cover authority expansion, map-bearing reset and unsupported transfer, candidate aliases, emergency-stop rejection, concurrent proposers, ABA recovery, epoch exhaustion, and owner teardown. Same-source Loom models check the invocation/transition race and guard lifetime. Cross-compilation and emulator dispatch are software checks; neither establishes physical safety or continual-learning performance.
 
-\section{Two complementary assurance timescales}
-\label{sec:requirements}
-\paragraph{Actions and installations.}
-Shielding can restrict or correct learner actions to satisfy a specification \citep{alshiekh2018shielding}. Neural Simplex combines runtime assurance with online retraining of a neural controller \citep{phan2020neural}. Safety layers have also been applied to robot foundation-model actions \citep{tolle2025safe}. We do not claim that learning and runtime safety have previously been disconnected. Action-level and update-level assurance can be complementary: one asks whether an action may execute in the current state; the other asks whether a particular artifact, with particular rights and state, may become active. Some systems can implement both. The distinction is an evaluation obligation, not an exclusive taxonomy of prior work.
+\section{Limits and research agenda}
+\label{sec:limits}
+The guarded protocol trades availability for an explicit failure mode: an invocation may be skipped during a transition, and a replacement may have to retry rather than wait. Whether that trade is acceptable depends on the slot period and the controller above it. Likewise, retaining both programs consumes memory, and rejecting map-bearing candidates excludes useful stateful updates. These are visible limits rather than silent state-transfer assumptions.
 
-Dynamic software updating supplies a second lineage. Kitsune supports updating running C programs and transforming their state \citep{hayden2014kitsune}. Live replacement and state migration are therefore not new ideas. Our proposed research agenda brings them together with agent-originated candidates, explicit actuator authority, continuing action assurance, and recovery that respects physical irreversibility. These requirements may also apply to human-authored updates; autonomous generation makes their repeated evaluation especially relevant.
+The manager campaign and component ablation can test exclusivity, freshness, fail-closed behavior, and accounting, but it cannot support claims about physical safety. A command already dispatched downstream may outlive the installation that produced it. Closing that gap requires carrying installation identity into command and actuation records and defining where stale commands are rejected. Hardware experiments must separately validate emergency-stop timing, plant assumptions, and safe re-arming.
 
-\paragraph{A candidate is a request, not a grant.}
-Represent a proposed update as
-\begin{equation}
- u_i=(id_i,\,artifact_i,\,authority_i,\,state_i,\,constraints_i).
-\end{equation}
-The identity binds immutable artifact bytes; authority denotes \emph{requested} rights, checked against the proposing principal's existing rights. State denotes an explicit reset or transfer policy, not a snapshot of the physical world. Constraints declare bounds that the receiving platform must validate. Authentication identifies bytes and their signer; it does not establish intent, controller stability, or permission to activate them.
-
-We propose five lifecycle obligations. \textbf{Admission} checks artifact validity, permissions, and resource requirements before granting active authority. \textbf{Staging} holds a candidate without enabling its outputs and accounts for coexistence with the current version. \textbf{Activation} has one declared publication point for an exclusive control slot; old invocations and queued commands cannot silently act as the new version. \textbf{State and recovery} specify reset or validated transfer, a failure detector, and bounded return to a declared operating state. Selecting prior code creates a fresh installation whose state and commands must be reconsidered. \textbf{Attribution} connects candidate identity, decisions, active version, and observed outcomes, with explicit missing-record reporting.
-
-Here atomicity concerns software authority at a defined executor boundary. It does not promise zero activation time, persistent storage, bumpless physical transfer, or database-style atomicity of effects. A trusted request monitor and a stop path must continue to constrain execution during changes. Their physical guarantees depend on the declared plant model, wiring, timing, and failure assumptions; these cannot be inferred from the installation protocol.
-
-\section{An incomplete case study and limited measurements}
-\label{sec:prototype}
-Our case study is an anonymized bare-metal Rust research kernel that admits verifier-accepted eBPF programs at runtime. It contains an authentication and authorization pipeline, verifier-produced executable objects, resource quotas, generation-tagged object handles, and immutable hook snapshots. Process capabilities can be restricted but not expanded through their capability interface. Actuation requests pass through an authority-aware monitor that can allow, clamp, force safe output, or reject. These are \textbf{implemented components}, not a demonstration of a continually learning agent.
-
